course: dictionary
lesson: 7461
title: Popularity: liked by many
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Popularity means being liked, enjoyed or supported by many people. It can also mean how many people use or choose something. Look at the words around it: «Number one means a song was bought, streamed or played most that week» tells you the number shows how many people chose a song.
explain: Popularity is not the same as quality. The sentence says it «shows popularity, not quality», and many songs critics love never reached number one. Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "Number one shows how many people chose the song" still makes sense.
explain: Popularity is often seen in school life and in charts. Phrases like «grow in popularity», «gain popularity» and «lose popularity» show that popularity can rise or fall. On a bar chart, the popularity of foods or pets is shown by the height of each bar.
explain: Watch out for the bandwagon trap, also called «everyone does it» or appeal to popularity. This is when people think something must be right just because many people do or say it. Kindness matters more than being popular, so ask yourself: is it right, or just popular?
fact: Popularity means being liked, enjoyed or supported by many people.
fact: Popularity can also mean how many people use or choose something, such as a sport.
fact: «Grow in popularity» means becoming more popular over time.
fact: A popularity contest is one where people vote for who or what they like best.
fact: The bandwagon trap treats popular as proof that something is right, but many people can still be wrong.
exercise: «Number one means a song was bought, streamed or played most that week.» What does number one show about a song? | *How many people chose it | Its length in minutes | Its quality, as critics see it | The name of the singer
exercise: Mia says, «Everyone in my class has this bag, so it must be the best one.» What trap is Mia falling into? | Context clues | A bar chart | A synonym | *The bandwagon trap
exercise: Which phrase means something becomes more liked over time? | *Grow in popularity | Bandwagon trap | Lose popularity | Barely a whisper
test: A bar chart shows the popularity of pets in your class. Which bar shows the most popular pet? | *The tallest bar | The shortest bar | The bar at the very end of the chart | The bar with the most letters on it
test: Which sentence shows the bandwagon trap? | I like the blue one best. | Thank you for the lovely gift. | The bar chart is shaded green. | *Everyone has one, so I should buy one too.
exam: Ella says a song must be great because it is number one. Why might Ella be wrong? | Number one means the song is quiet | Number one means the singer is famous for old songs | The song is the longest one in the chart | *Number one shows popularity, and popularity is not the same as quality
